\date{}
\begin{document}
\maketitle

\section{Introduction}
Tuwari (ISO 639-3: tww; Glottocode tuwa1242) belongs to the small Walio family (Walio, Pei, Yawiyo, Tuwari), spoken in the upper Walio River area of Papua New Guinea. Sources give about 330--360 speakers. Glottolog lists it as severely endangered. Sources disagree on the province (Sandaun or East Sepik) and on whether the Walio family is part of Sepik. Very little educational material was found in search results: no primers, textbooks, dictionaries or scripture were located.

\section{Primary Educational Resources}
No primers, textbooks or school programmes in Tuwari were found in search results.

\section{Linguistic and Reference Materials}
\begin{itemize}
\item Tuwari language (encyclopedic overview): \url{https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tuwari_language}
\item Walio languages (family overview): \url{https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Walio_languages}
\item Glottolog entry for Tuwari, which cites work by Loiseau on noun classes: \url{https://glottolog.org/resource/languoid/id/tuwa1242}
\item Numeral system entry for Tuwari (body-tally numerals): \url{https://lingweb.eva.mpg.de/channumerals/Tuwari.htm}
\item Endangered Languages Project entry: \url{https://endangeredlanguages.com/lang/3452}
\end{itemize}

\section{Religious and Community Resources}
Joshua Project gives a general ethnographic profile only (not a linguistic source): \url{https://joshuaproject.net/people_groups/15669}

\section{Digital and Audio Resources}
No audio, video or corpus resources for Tuwari were found in search results.

\section{Curriculum Development Context}
Documentation is minimal. Any curriculum would need new fieldwork with the community to produce an orthography, word lists and texts before teaching materials can be built. The reference items above provide only background and classification.
\end{document}
